# Globs # {#globs}

A <dfn export>glob</dfn> is a [=string=] matched against a URL. `*` matches zero
or more characters. Every other character matches only itself, so a glob
containing no wildcards matches only a URL identical to it.

Glob matching is case-sensitive.

<div class=issue>
The published specification states that a single `?` matches exactly one
character (added by [w3c/webextensions#542](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/pull/542),
"Add content scripts section in specification"). That text was written as
its author's own best understanding at the time, not as a group decision:
the pull request's body calls the section "a first draft," and its author
asked reviewers directly, "Would you be able to take a look at this one
and confirm if it is accurate? This was my best understanding based on
bugs and documentation in the code." Nobody confirmed it, and no other
discussion of `?` semantics exists anywhere in the group's record. It does
not match Chrome: Chrome treats a run of *k* consecutive `?` as matching 0
to *k* characters, so a lone `?` there can match zero characters, not
exactly one. Firefox compiles `?` to the regex `.`, which requires exactly
one character, matching the published text. Safari does not implement
glob matching at all, so it has no `?` semantics to compare.
</div>

<div class=issue>
Each browser handles escaping differently. Chrome treats `\` as an
escape character for the next `*` or `?`. Firefox has no escape syntax: a
`\` in a glob is matched as a literal backslash, and there is no way to
write a literal `*` or `?`. Safari does not implement glob matching.
</div>

<div class=issue>
Each browser matches a glob against a different string. Chrome matches
a glob against the full URL, including the [=url/fragment=]. Firefox matches
against the full URL with the [=url/fragment=] stripped. A glob ending in a
fragment, such as `*#top`, can therefore match in Chrome but never in
Firefox.
</div>

<div class="example">
Given the URL `https://example.com/path?query#frag`:

- `*example.com*` matches in both Chrome and Firefox.
- `*#frag` matches in Chrome, but not in Firefox, because Firefox matches against the URL with its fragment removed.

</div>

## Key `include_globs` ## {#globs-key-include_globs}

A <a data-link-type="dfn" data-link-spec="infra">list</a> of [=globs=] that a document should match. A document matches if the URL matches both the [`matches`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-content_scripts-matches) field and the `include_globs` field. If `include_globs` is empty or not present, it does not restrict matching.

Issue: Safari does not implement `include_globs`. The manifest key is
parsed and stored, but the actual injection-matching functions never
consult it. A content script that declares `include_globs` to narrow a
broad `matches` pattern will inject on the full, unnarrowed `matches` set
in Safari.

## Key `exclude_globs` ## {#globs-key-exclude_globs}

A <a data-link-type="dfn" data-link-spec="infra">list</a> of [=globs=] that can be used to specify URLs where the content script should not run, even if the URL matches entries in [`matches`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-content_scripts-matches) and (if specified) [[#globs-key-include_globs]]. If `exclude_globs` is empty or not present, it does not exclude anything.

Issue: Safari does not implement `exclude_globs`, for the same reason as
`include_globs` above.
